{"turn_id": "budget-8", "session_id": "mem-budget", "turn": 8, "answer": {"claims": [{"key": "twitter_fewer_missing_languages", "text": "The authors report that in countries where corpora do not represent all the languages produced by local populations, Twitter has fewer missing languages than the web-crawled data.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-9887004ca215701b79e8e570"}]}, {"key": "twitter_region_coverage", "text": "The authors state that Twitter provides a better representation of North America and Oceania than the web-crawled corpus.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-01b9a363a68a29a0e9af4ff8"}]}, {"key": "english_spanish_common_on_twitter", "text": "The authors note that English and Spanish are very common on Twitter, so countries predominantly using these languages will have good representation by default.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-01b9a363a68a29a0e9af4ff8"}]}, {"key": "twitter_gdp_weighting", "text": "The authors find Twitter data matches populations better when population numbers are weighted by GDP and worse when weighted by internet-usage statistics, which they interpret to imply Twitter represents more wealthy populations than web-crawled data.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-9887004ca215701b79e8e570"}]}, {"key": "urban_alternative_interpretation", "text": "The authors offer an alternate interpretation that their Twitter collection is based on urban areas, which tend to have more wealthy populations.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-9887004ca215701b79e8e570"}]}, {"key": "more_false_positives_twitter", "text": "The authors note that while false positives are uncommon overall, there are more false positives in Twitter data than in the web-crawled corpus.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-9887004ca215701b79e8e570"}]}, {"key": "false_positive_sources_unresolved", "text": "For Russia, the authors state that more research is needed to distinguish between immigration, tourism, and business as alternate sources of false positive languages.", "citations": [{"paper_id": "2004.00809", "chunk_id": "c-01b9a363a68a29a0e9af4ff8"}]}], "decision": "answer"}, "error": null, "trace_id": "budget-8", "assessment": {"execution_success": true, "task_success": null, "pipeline_success": null, "behavior_match": true, "schema_valid": true, "stage_flow_valid": true, "citation_structure_valid": true, "memory_committed": true, "nlu_correct": null, "reflect_correct": null, "retrieval_effective": true, "generate_valid": true, "degraded": false}}
